          Most caulk feels dry within an hour, and most caulk is nowhere near finished at that point. A bead cures from the outside in, so the skin you can touch hides a soft core that may need a day, three days or a full week to firm up. The right wait depends on the chemistry in the tube, the size of the bead and the air around it.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="three-clocks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Three Clocks Every Bead Runs On
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ask how long caulk takes to dry and you will get answers ranging from 30 minutes to a week, and every one of them can be right. That is because a bead of sealant passes three separate milestones, and labels, forums and hardware store staff often mean different ones when they say dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skin time</strong> is when a thin film forms on the surface. You can touch it lightly without leaving a fingerprint and dust will no longer stick. For most products this takes somewhere between 10 minutes and an hour. It is also the deadline for tooling: once a skin forms, pressing the bead wrinkles the surface instead of smoothing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water-ready or paint-ready time</strong> is the point at which the label says the bead can take light rain, a shower or a coat of paint without damage. It ranges from about 30 minutes for fast silicones to 24 hours for some latex and polymer products. <strong>Full cure</strong> is when the entire bead, including its core, has reached its final hardness and flexibility. That can take one to ten days or longer, depending on chemistry and thickness.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
