        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most jobs, plan on a skin within an hour, a rain or paint window of a few hours, and full cure in a few days to a week. Latex dries by losing water and likes warm, dry air; silicone, polyurethane and hybrids cure by absorbing moisture and slow down in very dry conditions. Bead depth matters as much as chemistry.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When the timing is tight, choose a fast-skinning hybrid or silicone and keep the bead modest. When it is not, give the joint an extra day. Nobody has ever regretted letting caulk cure too long, and plenty of people have cut out a bead that met water or paint too soon.
          </p>
        </div>

